WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.930
Producers &amp; cost curves

2
00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:09.440
Why does MC eventually rise?

3
00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:18.990
The law of diminishing returns — adding more of one input to fixed inputs eventually raises output by smaller and smaller amounts. Ten cooks, one oven.

4
00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:23.360
Economic vs. accounting profit?

5
00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:34.440
Economic profit subtracts all opportunity costs including the salary you gave up; accounting profit subtracts only explicit money costs. Economic ≤ accounting, always.

6
00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:40.190
Why does MC cut AVC and ATC at their minimums?

7
00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:48.460
An average falls while the next value is below it and rises once the next value is above it — so the curves cross exactly at the bottom.

8
00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:52.700
Shutdown vs. exit rule?

9
00:00:52.700 --> 00:01:02.900
Short run: shut down if P &lt; min AVC. Long run: exit if P &lt; min ATC. Breakeven price = min ATC, where economic profit is exactly zero.

10
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:07.780
The trap on computing marginals from a table?

11
00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:15.810
Divide by the change in Q. If output jumps 10 → 14, MC = ΔTC ÷ 4, not ΔTC.

12
00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:19.890
What is the firm’s supply curve?

13
00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:29.460
Its MC curve above the relevant shutdown point. Add firms horizontally for market supply; entry drives price to min long-run ATC and zero economic profit.

14
00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:33.540
What makes supply more elastic?

15
00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:44.020
Anything that makes the producer more flexible — inventories, non-perishability, the long run, easily adjusted variable inputs. Heavy fixed inputs make it less elastic.

16
00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.000
Producers &amp; cost curves — 7 cards
